WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:09.280
The bpx-palm-xx-palm-lib-member contains the parameters used to influence user logon,

2
00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:14.240
the maximum number of active processes, file handling and storage requirements

3
00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:16.960
for the zos-unix-system-services environment.

4
00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:21.600
These parameter values apply to all Unix processes in the system.

5
00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:27.280
Many bpx-palm-xx-parameter values can be modified without re-initializing the system

6
00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:33.360
using either the set-omvs or the set-omvs equals operator commands.

7
00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:40.560
You can control the amount of resources that are consumed by individual zos-unix-system-services

8
00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:46.800
users. bpx-palm-xx is still used to define resource limits, but it should be used to

9
00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:50.240
set the limits to the values required by the majority of users.

10
00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:56.640
The rackf-omvs segment of the user profile can be used to define the resource limits for

11
00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:01.280
users that have higher requirements than the limits defined in bpx-palm-xx.

12
00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:08.720
The add-user and alt-user commands can be used to specify or adjust the resource limits

13
00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:15.280
for an individual user. This diagram illustrates the read-only ZFS HFS data sharing which was the

14
00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:22.480
only supported file system data sharing for zos. ZOS provides support for simultaneous read-write

15
00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:28.560
access to the same ZFS or HFS file system across instances of Unix system services running

16
00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:34.800
on multiple ZOS images in a Sysplex. Only one Sysplex system owns a given file system.

17
00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:40.560
Across system coupling facility or XCF services are used during the processing of shared

18
00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:46.720
file systems in the Sysplex. With shared ZFS and HFS, all file systems that are mounted

19
00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:52.080
by a system participating in the shared HFS are available to all participating systems.

20
00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:55.760
In other words, once a file system is mounted by a participating system,

21
00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:00.480
that file system is accessible by any other participating system.

22
00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:06.960
Function shipping means that a request is forwarded to the owning system and the

23
00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:10.960
response is returned back to the requester through XCF communications.

24
00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:16.480
A file system can be mounted Sysplex aware or non-Sysplex aware.

25
00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:21.680
When a file system is mounted Sysplex aware, it means that the file system is locally

26
00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:26.480
mounted on every system when the PFS is capable of handling a local mount on every system,

27
00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:32.720
that is the PFS is running Sysplex aware, and therefore file requests are handled by the local

28
00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:38.560
PFS. All read-only mounted file systems are always mounted Sysplex aware.

29
00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:45.280
HFS read-write mounted file systems are always mounted non-Sysplex aware. This means that

30
00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:52.320
file requests from non-ZOS Unix owning systems are always function shipped by ZOS Unix to the

31
00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:58.160
ZOS Unix owning system where the file system is locally mounted and the IO is actually done.

32
00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:02.800
To enable ZFS Sysplex aware on a file system basis,

33
00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:09.840
specify Sysplex equals file sys in IOE PRMXX or IOE FS PRM.

34
00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:19.200
You can specify it in a shared IOE PRMXX, IOE FS PRM and each system picks up the specification

35
00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:26.400
in a rolling IPL. This means that USS sends all file requests directly to the local CFS

36
00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:31.600
physical file system. CFS direct IO is a performance

37
00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:37.120
improvement for the shared file system environment using ZFS read-write Sysplex aware file systems.

38
00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:43.040
ZFS direct IO enhances support for ZFS read-write Sysplex aware file systems.

39
00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:48.080
CFS can directly read and write Sysplex aware file systems from all systems in the shared

40
00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:52.880
file system environment. This makes file system ownership much less important.

41
00:03:54.320 --> 00:04:00.080
If the ZFS file system is mounted Sysplex aware, any client ZFS can directly access the file

42
00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:06.000
system for reading and writing data and partially also reading metadata. So any user or

43
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:11.280
application can exploit the functionality for ZFS read-write Sysplex aware file systems.

44
00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:19.680
In this case, the Sysplex aware file system FS2 is ZFS owned by SY2 and is being directly

45
00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:26.240
accessed from SY1 and SY3. Ownership will no longer move because we only keep count of metadata

46
00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:34.800
update. The system route is a ZFS HFS data set that is used as the Sysplex wide route.

47
00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:42.400
This ZFS HFS data set must be mounted read-write. Only one Sysplex route is allowed for all systems

48
00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:49.280
participating in shared HFS. Directories in the system specified HFS data set are used as mount

49
00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:58.480
points specifically for slash etc slash var slash temp and slash dev. And that wraps up this lecture

50
00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:04.720
series on COS Unix system services. I hope you found it useful and thank you for watching.
